blocks:
  - type: metin
    baslik: What is ADHD?
    metin: |-
      Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD) is a neurodevelopmental condition that affects a child's attention span, impulse control and activity level. It stems from differences in how the brain's executive functions develop — it is not the result of laziness, poor parenting or inadequate discipline.

      Symptoms typically begin before the age of 12 and are observed in at least two settings — for example, both at home and at school. Many children receive a diagnosis during primary school years, although some are not identified until adolescence.
  - type: bilgi-kutusu
    ton: yesil
    baslik: 'DSM-5 diagnostic criterion: the six-month rule'
    metin: An ADHD diagnosis requires that at least six symptoms of inattention or hyperactivity-impulsivity have persisted for at least six months, are inconsistent with the child's developmental level, and are impairing functioning across more than one setting.
  - type: kart-grid
    baslik: What do ADHD symptoms look like in children?
    giris: 'ADHD symptoms fall into three main groups. Not every child will show all three. This is part of what makes identification challenging:'
    kolon: 3
    kartlar:
      - baslik: Inattention
        maddeler:
          - Difficulty noticing details
          - Unable to sustain attention on tasks or play
          - Seems not to listen when spoken to directly
          - Fails to follow through on instructions
          - Frequently loses belongings
          - Easily distracted by external stimuli
          - Forgetful in daily activities
      - baslik: Hyperactivity
        maddeler:
          - Fidgets with hands or feet
          - Leaves seat when expected to remain seated
          - Runs or climbs in inappropriate situations
          - Difficulty playing quietly
          - Often "on the go" or driven by a motor
          - Talks excessively
      - baslik: Impulsivity
        maddeler:
          - Blurts out answers before a question is finished
          - Can't wait their turn
          - Interrupts or intrudes on others
          - Acts without thinking about consequences
          - Extreme emotional reactions
  - type: kart-yigini
    baslik: What parents and teachers typically notice
    giris: 'ADHD presents differently depending on the setting. Here is what is most commonly reported:'
    kartlar:
      - baslik: At home
        metin: Getting up repeatedly while doing homework, being able to sit at a screen for hours but unable to hold a book for five minutes, constantly losing things, difficulty remembering household rules, sudden conflicts with siblings.
      - baslik: At school
        metin: Struggling to follow lessons, copying from classmates, inability to remain seated, difficulty waiting their turn, friction in group work, poor use of exam time.
      - baslik: In social situations
        metin: Disregarding or quickly breaking the rules of a game, fragile friendships, sudden outbursts of anger, very low tolerance for frustration.
  - type: bilgi-kutusu
    ton: yesil
    baslik: 'A common misconception: "But they can play video games for hours"'
    metin: Children with ADHD can focus intensely on activities they find highly engaging; this is called hyperfocus. It does not rule out ADHD; rather, it reflects how their brain's reward system works. The core difficulty is not an absence of attention but an inability to direct attention voluntarily.
  - type: kart-yigini
    baslik: Types of ADHD
    kartlar:
      - baslik: Predominantly Inattentive Type
        metin: Hyperactivity is not prominent; the child may appear calm or even dreamy. More commonly seen in girls, which means diagnosis is often delayed.
      - baslik: Predominantly Hyperactive-Impulsive Type
        metin: Motor restlessness and impulse control are more prominent than attention difficulties. More frequently seen in boys and more likely to be identified early.
      - baslik: Combined Type
        metin: Both symptom groups are present and significant. This is the most common presentation of ADHD.
